FAQ = [
 ("Will DT running boards fit a Ram 1500 Classic?",
  "No. Ram sold the new DT 1500 and the older 1500 Classic side by side from 2019 to 2024, and they have different bodies and rocker mounting points. Go Rhino's titles say 'New Body Style Only. Excludes Classic Body Style', and Westin's say 'Exc 1500 Classic'. The interior is the quick check: most DTs have a rotary gear selector on the dash."),
 ("Crew Cab or Quad Cab?",
  "Every pick here is Crew Cab. The DT Quad Cab has shorter rear doors and needs shorter boards; Go Rhino and Westin sell Quad Cab versions under different part numbers."),
 ("What's the difference between the RB20 and RB20 Slim?",
  "RealTruck lists the standard RB20 at 7.5 in wide at the front and 6.5 in at the rear, and the RB20 Slim at a 5.5 in step. The Slim sits narrower for a cleaner look and more side clearance; the standard gives more foot room. Both are galvanized 16-gauge steel with the same warranty terms."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install?",
  "Not usually. RealTruck lists the Go Rhino RB20 and RB20 Slim as no-drill bolt-on installs, and Westin says its PRO TRAXX bars are bolt-on with no drilling on most applications."),
 ("What does a wheel-to-wheel nerf bar add?",
  "A step alongside the bed, from the front wheel well to the rear one. Westin's PRO TRAXX 5 21-534725 is a wheel-to-wheel bar for the DT Crew Cab with Westin's '6 ft 5 in' bed listing. It helps you reach into the bed or a RamBox."),
 ("Do these fit the TRX or RHO?",
  "The TRX and RHO have wider bodywork and different rocker treatment on some years. Most listings name the Crew Cab without trim exclusions, but confirm with the seller for a TRX or RHO before ordering."),
 ("How much weight can boards hold?",
  "It depends on the product. RealTruck lists the Go Rhino RB30, a close sibling of the RB20, at 600 lb per side. Budget listings often state a rating in the description; ask if it isn't there."),
 ("Do drop steps help on a lifted Ram?",
  "Yes. Drop steps put a lower step at each door, which helps on a lifted truck or for short passengers. SMANOW's drop-down steps are listed for the DT Crew Cab. They reduce side clearance, so they're a road-truck choice."),
 ("Can I install them myself?",
  "Yes, with a helper and basic tools. Bolt the brackets to the rocker points, set the boards, level them, torque the bolts and recheck after a week."),
 ("What about factory power steps?",
  "Higher DT trims offer factory power running boards. Aftermarket boards replace them rather than bolting alongside."),
 ("Do running boards interfere with the Ram's air suspension?",
  "Ram's optional air suspension lowers the truck for entry on some trims. Running boards bolt to the body's rocker points, so they move with the body, not the axles. When the truck lowers, the board gets closer to the ground, which matters on steep driveways and curbs. Choose a slimmer board or a nerf bar if clearance is tight at your home."),
 ("Should I pick the bedliner or textured black finish on the RB20?",
  "Both finishes sit over galvanized steel. The bedliner coating is rougher and more chip-resistant, which suits gravel roads and work use. The textured black powder coat looks smoother and more finished. RealTruck lists the same structural and finish warranty terms for the RB20 either way."),
]

